John Edward: Global Citizens Need Language Skills
Independent schools in Scotland are offering excellent education, which has once again been proven with excellent test results in 2018, in which skills have been reinforced in sports, arts, music and other social activities as an individual and team.
These schools, which have over 30,000 people across Slotland, set up The Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) to provide the best services to their students and their parents.
Independent schools want their students to get more higher education, shape their daily lives brilliantly and turn them into Global Citizens.
In the academic discipline aimed at uncovering the talents of the students, modern languages in the school are being chosen as the curriculum that is popular and the more people want.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you talk to a man in a language he understands, it goes to his brain."
If that's the case in his mother tongue, it goes into his heart."
Therefore, this is an example of how we can't build relationships with other countries based solely on English.
The results of this year's exam have shown that the students in independent schools have chosen the languages as their main curriculum.
About 68 per cent of those who are studying foreign languages have achieved higher grade A.
According to data collected from SCIS schools with 74 members, 72 per cents got Mandarin Higher Grade A, 72 per cent of those studying in German, 69 percent of those who studied French, and 63 per cent of those studying in Spanish.
It is believed that Scotland's independent schools provide knowledge of foreign languages that will be useful for children and young people in the future.
In Independent Schools Curriculums and elsewhere have now become a curriculum that can be chosen like STEM curricula (Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics).
According to a survey by the UK Commission in 2014 regarding jobs and skills, up to 17 per cent of the reasons for non-compliation of vacancies is the primary.
Language skills are becoming increasingly important for the future.
Language skills are very important in a globalized world.
If you have a second language, it's going to be very useful.
Individuals who are able to communicate directly in their language with those who come from abroad will definitely advance in the competition.
According to the YouGov survey conducted by 4,000 adults in the UK in 2013, 75 per cents are unable to speak a foreign language, while only 15 per cents are able to speak French.
That's why it's so important for children to learn different languages.
Learning multiple languages, especially from developing countries, has the potential to provide better employment to children.
There are different languages taught in every school in Scotland.
In most schools, more classic modern languages are being taught, while others are being taught, such as Japanese or Mandarin, which is necessary for the UK to focus on 2020.
No matter what your child chooses, experts in the area will be available in independent schools to teach those languages.
Scotland's independent schools provide all sorts of services to prepare their children to cope with all the challenges they face in the future.
At the moment it may be rejected, but languages in the field of global business are going to be very important for the future of the country, so they must be included in education.
Modern languages must be considered "international communication skills."
Independent schools for the youth of Scotland must offer this option, diversity and expertise.
It's a faut bien le faire.
John Edward, director of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
